From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Joonhoe Kim <26rote@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 3 Oct 2026 10:24:37 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] cpuidle: psci-domain: add allow_cluster_off to refuse cluster
 idle states

On the Lenovo TB323FU (SM8850) the tablet still resets now and then
while it sits idle, with the system domain state already kept for
system suspend only: CPUs of cluster0 enter PSCI CPU_SUSPEND and never
return, and the losses line up with cluster0 being turned off and on by
the last CPU of the cluster going idle.

With a test change that made psci_pd_power_off() return -EBUSY for the
cluster domains, the tablet ran 3 hours idle without a crash (562364
refused cluster0 power-offs), against two crashes in 78 minutes with the
cluster states allowed. At that rate of about 1.5 crashes per hour, 3
crash-free hours would happen by chance about 1% of the time. Power with
the display off, cluster states allowed / refused / allowed: 658 / 659 /
621 mW, within the measurement noise of about 20 mW.

Add a module parameter, allow_cluster_off, default Y (the current
behaviour). When it is N, psci_pd_power_off() refuses the idle states of
cluster domains in runtime idle. A cluster domain is one whose
subdomains have no subdomains of their own, i.e. the parent of CPU
domains; the CPU domains and the system domain above the clusters are
not affected. s2idle still uses the cluster states, because the system
domain state (CX and DDR collapse) can only be entered once the clusters
are off. The parameter can be changed at run time through
/sys/module/cpuidle_psci_domain/parameters/allow_cluster_off.

The baldur config fragment sets cpuidle_psci_domain.allow_cluster_off=0
on the built-in command line.

This is a workaround: it avoids the symptom by not entering the cluster
states in runtime idle, the cause is not fixed. It is not known whether
the cause is a race around cluster off/on in the OSI idle path or a
defect in the firmware (PSCI implementation or hypervisor). s2idle still
enters the cluster states and is not covered by the measurement above.

Signed-off-by: Joonhoe Kim <26rote@gmail.com>

[mzak: driver part only, ported by hand (include context); the baldur.fragment
 hunk is not taken: iceland keeps the default allow_cluster_off=Y, i.e. the
 behaviour is unchanged unless the parameter is cleared at run time]

Signed-off-by: mzak <mzak.amin@gmail.com>
---
 drivers/cpuidle/cpuidle-psci-domain.c | 21 +++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 21 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/cpuidle/cpuidle-psci-domain.c b/drivers/cpuidle/cpuidle-psci-domain.c
index 5722365b943e..da036930934c 100644
--- a/drivers/cpuidle/cpuidle-psci-domain.c
+++ b/drivers/cpuidle/cpuidle-psci-domain.c
@@ -12,12 +12,14 @@
 #include <linux/cpu.h>
 #include <linux/device.h>
 #include <linux/kernel.h>
+#include <linux/moduleparam.h>
 #include <linux/platform_device.h>
 #include <linux/pm_domain.h>
 #include <linux/pm_runtime.h>
 #include <linux/psci.h>
 #include <linux/slab.h>
 #include <linux/string.h>
+#include <linux/suspend.h>
 
 #include "cpuidle-psci.h"
 #include "dt_idle_genpd.h"
@@ -29,11 +31,30 @@ struct psci_pd_provider {
 
 static LIST_HEAD(psci_pd_providers);
 
+static bool allow_cluster_off = true;
+module_param(allow_cluster_off, bool, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(allow_cluster_off,
+		 "Use the idle states of cluster domains (parents of CPU domains) in runtime idle (default: Y)");
+
+static bool psci_pd_is_cluster(struct generic_pm_domain *pd)
+{
+	struct gpd_link *link;
+
+	link = list_first_entry_or_null(&pd->parent_links, struct gpd_link,
+					parent_node);
+	return link && list_empty(&link->child->parent_links);
+}
+
 static int psci_pd_power_off(struct generic_pm_domain *pd)
 {
 	struct genpd_power_state *state = &pd->states[pd->state_idx];
 	u32 *pd_state;
 
+	/* s2idle needs the cluster states to reach the system domain state. */
+	if (!READ_ONCE(allow_cluster_off) && !idle_should_enter_s2idle() &&
+	    psci_pd_is_cluster(pd))
+		return -EBUSY;
+
 	if (!state->data)
 		return 0;
 
-- 
2.43.0

